 ## Overview
-Gradient-Based Surface Normal Estimation computes surface normals for a volumetric representation by evaluating the spatial gradient of a local density field (resembling a signed distance field) and normalizing the resulting vector [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of deriving shading normals for voxelized or implicit geometry without storing explicit normal textures or relying on mesh topology [S1, unofficial], distinguishing itself from [Scene-Space Normal Estimation](10497-scene-space-normal-estimation.md) by operating in volume space and from [Tetrahedral Normal Estimation](1986-tetrahedral-normal-estimation.md) by using axis-aligned finite differences rather than geometric interpolation [G].
+Gradient-Based Surface Normal Estimation computes surface normals for a volumetric representation by evaluating the spatial gradient of a local density field and normalizing the resulting vector [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It solves the problem of deriving shading normals for voxelized or implicit geometry without storing explicit normal textures or relying on mesh topology [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling methods that may use pre-computed normal maps or tetrahedral interpolation, this approach derives normals on the fly from the scalar field itself, ensuring coherence with the voxelized volume resolution [S1, unofficial].
 
